---
title: Models of normals as least squares
part: estimation
order: 3
summary: When every term of a model is a normal with a fixed scale and a mean computed from other quantities, its negative log-density is half a sum of squared whitened residuals plus the charts' log-volumes and a constant, so its mode is a nonlinear least-squares problem; a scale that is estimated breaks the form.
depends_on: [normal, map-estimation]
---

<script>
	import Statement from '$lib/components/Statement.svelte';
	import Proof from '$lib/components/Proof.svelte';
	import * as leastSquares from './statements/least-squares.md';
</script>

The [mode](../map-estimation/) of a posterior is the minimizer of a sum of terms, one for each of the model's kernels.
For one family of models, by far the most common in estimation, each term is a square.
A quantity measured with Gaussian noise, a state that follows another up to Gaussian noise, a parameter with a Gaussian prior: each is a [normal](../normal/) whose mean is computed from other quantities, and a model built of such terms, its means depending on quantities that are themselves scored by normals, is hierarchical in the plainest sense.
This note shows that its mode is a *nonlinear least-squares problem*, says what the problem keeps of the density besides its squares, and where the form breaks.

## A sum of squares, and what else

<Statement {...leastSquares} />

<Proof>

By [](eq:math/map-estimation/sum), $f$ is the sum of the terms' negative log-densities.
By [](eq:math/normal/log-density), the term of a normal scoring $\point_{s_t}$ with mean $\mu_t$ and whitening $W_t$ is

$$
	-\logterm_{\mathcal N} = \tfrac12 \lvert r_t \rvert^2 - \sum_i \log (W_t)_{ii} + \tfrac{d_t}{2} \log 2\pi + \lambda_{\mu_t}(\point_{s_t} \ominus \mu_t) ,
$$

with $d_t$ the dimension of the quantity's space.
The scale is fixed, so the middle two summands are constants.

</Proof>

The objective is written, from here on, as

$$
	f(\point) = \sum_t \Bigl( \tfrac12 \lvert r_t(\point) \rvert^2 + \lambda_t(\point) \Bigr) + \text{a constant}, @tag(objective)
$$

a sum over *terms* $t$, each with a smooth *whitened residual* $r_t$, of any number of components, and a smooth scalar $\lambda_t$, which for many terms is zero, over a point $\point = (\point_1, \dots, \point_n)$ of a [product](../product-manifold/) of manifolds with retractions, the free quantities.
Each term reads only a few of the factors of $\point$, its *variables* $K_t$: the quantity it scores, if that is free, and the free quantities its mean is computed from.
The notes that follow use only this form, and the terms need not come from normals: any function of this shape is minimized by the same methods.

**The squares are what least squares sees.**
A residual is the difference between a quantity and the mean the model predicts for it, in the coordinates of the chart at the mean and in units of its noise.
Its square is the measurement's disagreement with the model, and the sum of squares is what an engineer would write down as the cost of a fit without any probability at all.
Minimizing it alone is the classical problem.

**The log-volumes are what make $f$ a negative log-density.**
The log-volume $\lambda_t$ is zero on a Euclidean space.
On the positive orthant it is $\sum_i \log \point_{s_t, i}$, which depends on the scored quantity and not only on its residual.
On a curved space, such as the rotations with the Cayley chart, it is a function of the residual's coordinates, of the relative order of the noise's variance near a zero residual.
Leaving the log-volumes out minimizes the sum of squares alone, which is the mode of [another model](../normal/#the-gaussian-part-alone), and keeping them finds the mode of this one against the reference measure of its spaces.
On Euclidean spaces the two coincide.

**A residual of no components.**
A term may have $r_t$ of no components and a nonzero $\lambda_t$: a factor that adds a function of the point and no square.
It fits the form, and the methods of the following notes carry its gradient exactly.

## Where the form breaks

The form rests on the normalizers being constant, and so on the scales being fixed.
A scale that is itself a free quantity breaks it in two places.
The normalizer $-\sum_i \log (W_t)_{ii}$ is then a function of the point, and so is the residual, through $W_t$.
For an isotropic scale $\sigma$, in its logarithmic chart $u = \log \sigma$, the residual is $e^{-u} (\point_{s_t} \ominus \mu_t)$ and the normalizer is $d_t u$: the first falls as the scale grows and the second rises.
Such an objective is still a sum of squares and a smooth remainder, and the methods of the following notes still apply to it, but the remainder is now as large as the squares near the mode, and the methods that neglect the curvature of the squares' residuals lose their reason.
A scale is estimated by a curvature that keeps its share, the expected information, which a later note takes up.
